Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable

<div dir=3D"ltr"><div><div>Hi David,</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks for ta=
king the time to read this. Some responses below.</div><div><br></div><div>=
<br></div><div>&gt; 1-Byte Length of Vectors: One edge-case that is implied=
 and not</div><div>&gt; discussed in the Presentation Language section is: =
&quot;do I need to write</div><div>&gt; the length of a vector&lt;0...255&g=
t;?&quot;. It seems logical that it will</div><div>&gt; always be 1 byte an=
d that it might be an implicit length. It would be</div><div>&gt; nice to h=
ave this edge case mentioned or say something along the lines</div><div>&gt=
; &quot;there are no exceptions when encoding the length of variable-length=
</div><div>&gt; vectors&quot;.</div><div><br></div><div>I&#39;m not sure I =
understand your point here about why this is an edge</div><div>case. In ord=
er for the encoding to be unambiguous, every variable</div><div>length vect=
ory must always be prefixed by the length, even if in</div><div>a particula=
r message it is zero length. This section seems pretty</div><div>clear to m=
e. Note that unconditional statement about encoding</div><div>followed by t=
he last sentence about empty vectors.</div><div><br></div><div>=C2=A0 =C2=
=A0Variable-length vectors are defined by specifying a subrange of legal</d=
iv><div>=C2=A0 =C2=A0lengths, inclusively, using the notation &lt;floor..ce=
iling&gt;.=C2=A0 When</div><div>=C2=A0 =C2=A0these are encoded, the actual =
length precedes the vector&#39;s contents</div><div>=C2=A0 =C2=A0in the byt=
e stream.=C2=A0 The length will be in the form of a number</div><div>=C2=A0=
 =C2=A0consuming as many bytes as required to hold the vector&#39;s specifi=
ed</div><div>=C2=A0 =C2=A0maximum (ceiling) length.=C2=A0 A variable-length=
 vector with an actual</div><div>=C2=A0 =C2=A0length field of zero is refer=
red to as an empty vector.</div><div><br></div><div>Happy to look at making=
 this clearer, but I&#39;d rather not just</div><div>add a clarifying sente=
nce.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>&gt; Lack of repetition: The i=
ntro diagram tells you what messages are</div><div>&gt; encrypted with what=
 key. But after that, no reminders are present. For</div><div>&gt; example,=
 look at the Finished message. How do you know that this has</div><div>&gt;=
 to be encrypted with the handshake key? It is not written in the</div><div=
>&gt; Finished message section, it is not written in the authentication</di=
v><div>&gt; messages parent section and it is not written in the handshake<=
/div><div>&gt; protocol parent-parent section either. Only in the overview =
section.</div><div><br></div><div>I&#39;ll see what I can do about this. Wi=
th that said, I&#39;m trying to remove</div><div>unnecessary duplication, s=
o it&#39;s a compromise.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>&gt; How t=
o define application data in an application data message: It is</div><div>&=
gt; not clear how &quot;real&quot; application data should appear in an app=
lication</div><div>&gt; data message. Encrypted handshakes and alerts are c=
learly defined, but</div><div>&gt; it takes some time to understand that &q=
uot;real&quot; application data is</div><div>&gt; written -- without struct=
ure or metadata -- into the content field of</div><div>&gt; a TLSInnerPlain=
text message. The current definition is &quot;content: The</div><div>&gt; c=
leartext of TLSPlaintext.fragment&quot;. I think a better one would be</div=
><div>&gt; &quot;content: the byte encoding of a handshake or alert message=
, or the</div><div>&gt; raw bytes of the application&#39;s data to send&quo=
t;.</div><div><br></div><div>Yeah, this seems like a good change. Feel free=
 to send a PR or I can just</div><div>do it.</div><div><br></div><div><br><=
/div><div>&gt; Unencrypted Application Data: Does it say somewhere in the</=
div><div>&gt; specification that real application data should never appear =
in a</div><div>&gt; TLSPlaintext message? At the moment the application_dat=
a content type</div><div>&gt; is present in the declaration of a TLSPlainte=
xt structure, see the</div><div>&gt; record layer section</div><div><br></d=
iv><div>I&#39;m not sure I understand what you&#39;re saying here.</div><di=
v><br></div><div><br></div><div>&gt; What alert to use: Sometimes it is not=
 mentioned what level of alert</div><div>&gt; should be used. For example: =
&quot;Because TLS 1.3 forbids renegotiation,</div><div>&gt; if a server rec=
eives a ClientHello at any other time, it MUST</div><div>&gt; terminate the=
 connection&quot;. I&#39;m guessing it should be a fatal alert,</div><div>&=
gt; but this is open to interpretation.</div><div><br></div><div>Hmm... I t=
hink this is pretty clear in 6.2.</div><div><br></div><div>=C2=A0 =C2=A0Whe=
never an implementation encounters a fatal error condition, it</div><div>=
=C2=A0 =C2=A0SHOULD send an appropriate fatal alert and MUST close the conn=
ection</div><div>=C2=A0 =C2=A0without sending or receiving any additional d=
ata.=C2=A0 In the rest of</div><div>=C2=A0 =C2=A0this specification, the ph=
rase &quot;{terminate the connection, abort the</div><div>=C2=A0 =C2=A0hand=
shake}&quot; is used without a specific alert means that the</div><div>=C2=
=A0 =C2=A0implementation SHOULD send the alert indicated by the description=
s</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>&gt; Why force the signature algo=
rithm extension: If the client does not</div><div>&gt; send a signature alg=
orithm extension, why would the server abort the</div><div>&gt; handshake? =
It would make more sense to continue with the server&#39;s</div><div>&gt; p=
refered certificate. Especially since currently, most webservers only</div>=
<div>&gt; possess one kind of certificate anyway.</div><div><br></div><div>=
The signature algorithms extension also indicates what to sign with,</div><=
div>and that&#39;s not completely determined by the certificate (e.g., with=
</div><div>RSA).</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>&gt; Resumption_se=
cret: The Key Schedule section mentions a</div><div>&gt; resumption_secret =
but nowhere in the spec does this keyword</div><div>&gt; re-appear. It shou=
ld be mentioned somewhere that this is the secret to</div><div>&gt; be used=
 as PSK in a future resumption handshake.</div><div><br></div><div>Good cat=
ch. This is actually called the resumption master</div><div>secret. I&#39;l=
l clean this up.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>&gt; Auth_modes: t=
here is a single mention of &quot;auth_modes&quot; in the</div><div>&gt; Pr=
e-Shared Key Extension section. No definition of this variable is to</div><=
div>&gt; be found.</div><div><br></div><div>Yeah, this is a holdover from w=
hen this field exists. I fixed that.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><di=
v><br></div><div>&gt; Computing Finished keys: The section on how to comput=
e Finished keys</div><div>&gt; should be in the cryptographic computation s=
ection in my opinion. It</div><div>&gt; should probably be added to the key=
 schedule diagram as well.</div><div><br></div><div>This made the key sched=
ule too hard to read (I note you complain</div><div>about that below) so I&=
#39;m reluctant to add it. I&#39;d be interested</div><div>in other people&=
#39;s opinions about whether these computations would</div><div>be better i=
n this section.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>&gt; Computation of=
 the Binders: From the relevant section: &quot;Each entry in</div><div>&gt;=
 the binders list is computed as an HMAC over the portion of the</div><div>=
&gt; ClientHello up to and including the PreSharedKeyExtension.identities</=
div><div>&gt; field. That is, it includes all of the ClientHello but not th=
e binder</div><div>&gt; list itself. The length fields for the message (inc=
luding the overall</div><div>&gt; length, the length of the extensions bloc=
k, and the length of the</div><div>&gt; =E2=80=9Cpre_shared_key=E2=80=9D ex=
tension) are all set as if the binder were</div><div>&gt; present&quot;. Th=
ere are several things that are unclear to me and I think</div><div>&gt; th=
ey should be mentioned here: the fake binder should be of size</div><div>&g=
t; hash.size, it should also say that the HMAC is computed over the</div><d=
iv>&gt; handshake message containing the client hello, not only the client<=
/div><div>&gt; hello structure.</div><div><br></div><div>I&#39;ll see what =
I can do here.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>&gt; What binder key=
 to use: It is not clear which binder are to be used</div><div>&gt; when do=
ing a session resumption. The previous handshake one or the new</div><div>&=
gt; handshake one? The confusion comes from the fact that the old</div><div=
>&gt; handshake&#39;s resumption_secret/PSK is to be used in the new handsh=
ake,</div><div>&gt; it could be thought that the old handshake&#39;s binder=
 key should be the</div><div>&gt; one used in the new handshake as well. I =
think it would help if this</div><div>&gt; resumption_secret is called some=
thing like</div><div>&gt; next_handshake.resumption_secret. Or make it clea=
rer from which key</div><div>&gt; schedule the binder key comes from.</div>=
<div><br></div><div>I&#39;m not quite following. It&#39;s not possible to c=
ompute the RMS</div><div>at this point in the handshake. The only PSK you c=
ould possibly</div><div>have is the one from the previous handshake.</div><=
div><br></div><div><br></div><div>&gt; PSK and Cipher Suite: I think the fo=
llowing fact should be emphasized</div><div>&gt; in more places: the client=
 needs to advertise the single cipher suite</div><div>&gt; they previously =
used when doing session resumption. It could be added</div><div>&gt; to the=
 cipher_suite definition of the client hello section as well as</div><div>&=
gt; in the overview of session resumption.</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks.=
 I can add something.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>&gt; Implicit=
 Lengths in The Presentation Language: nested structures +</div><div>&gt; i=
mplicit lengths =3D trouble. It&#39;s easy to forget parsing/writing all</d=
iv><div>&gt; the different &quot;sizes&quot; when they are not clearly stat=
ed in the</div><div>&gt; spec. Why not just having a length field in front =
of these</div><div>&gt; variable-length vectors? It would add a bit of over=
head but would make</div><div>&gt; things clearer in my opinion.</div><div>=
<br></div><div>Can you point to the specific structures you are concerned w=
ith</div><div>here? I think in almost every case we do have length fields. =
Actually,</div><div>a common complaint is we have too many redundant length=
 fields.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>&gt; No Help For The Imple=
menter: If you look at the Noise Protocol</div><div>&gt; Framework, you can=
 clearly see that the specifications were written</div><div>&gt; with imple=
menters in mind: several states, and objects are</div><div>&gt; defined. Fo=
r example, one way of implementing TLS is to have some sort</div><div>&gt; =
of complex state that will remember what state you are in the</div><div>&gt=
; handshake, what is the cryptographic context, etc... here the</div><div>&=
gt; implementer has to decide everything for himself.</div><div><br></div><=
div>Most IETF specs leave the choice of how to do things up</div><div>to th=
e implementor. As someone who has implemented a lot of</div><div>specs, I a=
ctually find it pretty frustrating when the author</div><div>gets all presc=
riptive about implementation. If there are</div><div>points that are unclea=
r, I think it&#39;s fine to make them clear,</div><div>but I&#39;d rather s=
tick to the PDUs.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>&gt; Whitelist Of=
 State Transitions: There is nothing about whitelisting</div><div>&gt; stat=
e transitions. In my opinion there should be a list of allowed</div><div>&g=
t; state transitions that the implementer could use. I made one here, I</di=
v><div>&gt; suspect it is still missing some states.</div><div><br></div><d=
iv>I&#39;m going to add a state diagram in -19. This link seems like</div><=
div>useful input. Thanks.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div=
><div>&gt; How to parse extensions: I think that IANA&#39;s consideration s=
ection&#39;s</div><div>&gt; table would be useful to have in the earlier Ex=
tension section</div><div>&gt; (probably only the extensions included in th=
e RFC should be</div><div>&gt; listed). Another useful pragraph: how to par=
se these extensions,</div><div>&gt; making sure that different messages com=
e with their own whitelist of</div><div>&gt; extensions.</div><div><br></di=
v><div>Good point. I&#39;ll make this change.</div><div><br></div><div><br>=
</div><div>&gt; Language &lt;-&gt; Bytes Translation: There are no real int=
roduction/no</div><div>&gt; clear section about this. I understand that eve=
rything is in the</div><div>&gt; Presentation Language section but for some=
one who discovers the</div><div>&gt; specification it takes some time to fi=
nd out that this section is</div><div>&gt; about both the presentation lang=
uage and its byte encoding. A better</div><div>&gt; introduction in this se=
ction as well as an emphasis on the byte</div><div>&gt; representation in e=
ach of the subsections of this section would in my</div><div>&gt; opinion b=
enefit the whole document.</div><div><br></div><div>I&#39;m not quite sure =
I understand what you&#39;re looking for here. Can</div><div>you send a PR?=
</div><div><br></div><div>&gt; Asymmetrical MUSTs: some &quot;MUST&quot; ke=
ywords are not symmetrical. What is</div><div>&gt; the point of them? For e=
xample, the spec says that as a client, you</div><div>&gt; MUST use a uniqu=
e 0x0303 value in the legacy_version field of the</div><div>&gt; client hel=
lo. But if you do not, there are no problems from the server</div><div>&gt;=
 side in the specification and the client hello will still be well</div><di=
v>&gt; received. (Is this maybe for forward compatibility?)</div><div><br><=
/div><div>Amongst other things. MUST do X but must allow X and Y is pretty<=
/div><div>common in IETF spec.</div><div><br></div><div>&gt; And finally, h=
ere are some minor nitpicks:</div><div>&gt;=C2=A0</div><div>&gt; PSK extens=
ion: debatable, but I would rather have the PSK section at</div><div>&gt; t=
he very end of the Extensions section as it MUST be the final</div><div>&gt=
; extension of a client hello.</div><div><br></div><div>Yeah, I can do that=
.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>&gt; No Indication Of Encryption:=
 This is debatable, but I find it</div><div>&gt; inelegant that there are n=
o indication in a record that it is</div><div>&gt; encrypted. The implement=
ation is supposed to keep track of it.</div><div><br></div><div>Actually, a=
t present everything with first byte =3D=3D application_data</div><div>is e=
ncrypted and everything else is not.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><di=
v>&gt; What PSK mode does a server support: I think the server should</div>=
<div>&gt; indicate what mode (psk_dh_ke or psk_ke) it supports in a</div><d=
iv>&gt; server_hello.psk_mode extension or in the new_session_ticket</div><=
div>&gt; message. Like that the client would know if it has to send a key_s=
hare</div><div>&gt; or not in the resumption. Right now, the only indicatio=
n of a</div><div>&gt; psk_dh_ke is that the server will send a key_share ev=
en when presented</div><div>&gt; with a PSK. this is debatable.</div><div><=
br></div><div>This was a pretty explicit decision to avoid redundancy, so I=
 think</div><div>we have WG consensus here.</div><div><br></div><div><br></=
div><div>&gt; The Key Derivation: The key derivation section is the least c=
lear</div><div>&gt; section in this specification. The diagrams kind of hel=
p, but are</div><div>&gt; limited by the RFC format... I have nothing else =
to say here, the</div><div>&gt; whole section is confusing to say the least=
. No solution offered :o)</div><div><br></div><div>Yeah, not too sure what =
to do.</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks,</div><div>-Ekr</div><div><br></div>=
</div><div class=3D"gmail_extra"><br><div class=3D"gmail_quote">On Wed, Dec=
 14, 2016 at 3:38 PM, David Wong <span dir=3D"ltr">&lt;<a href=3D"mailto:da=
vidwong.crypto@gmail.com" target=3D"_blank">davidwong.crypto@gmail.com</a>&=
gt;</span> wrote:<br><blockquote class=3D"gmail_quote" style=3D"margin:0px =
0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex"><div=
 dir=3D"ltr"><div id=3D"gmail-m_6082903737410361506gmail-readme" class=3D"g=
mail-m_6082903737410361506gmail-readme gmail-m_6082903737410361506gmail-blo=
b gmail-m_6082903737410361506gmail-instapaper_body">
    <article class=3D"gmail-m_6082903737410361506gmail-markdown-body gmail-=
m_6082903737410361506entry-content"><p>Hello!
 I read the current specification and I have some feedback on it. I hope
 this can be useful. Sorry for the length or if I point to obvious=20
things. Here is a link as well for readability: <a href=3D"https://gist.git=
hub.com/mimoo/b6c64a133cd85da53f2f744dcdb39839" target=3D"_blank">https://g=
ist.github.com/mimoo/<wbr>b6c64a133cd85da53f2f744dcdb398<wbr>39</a><br></p>

<ol><li><p><strong>1-Byte Length of Vectors</strong>: One edge-case that is=
 implied and not discussed in the <a href=3D"https://tlswg.github.io/tls13-=
spec/#presentation-language" target=3D"_blank">Presentation Language sectio=
n</a>
 is: &quot;do I need to write the length of a vector&lt;0...255&gt;?&quot;.=
 It=20
seems logical that it will always be 1 byte and that it might be an=20
implicit length. It would be nice to have this edge case mentioned or=20
say something along the lines &quot;there are no exceptions when encoding t=
he
 length of variable-length vectors&quot;.</p></li><li><p><strong>Lack of re=
petition</strong>: <a href=3D"https://tlswg.github.io/tls13-spec/#rfc.secti=
on.2" target=3D"_blank">The intro diagram</a>
 tells you what messages are encrypted with what key. But after that, no
 reminders are present. For example, look at the Finished message. How=20
do you know that this has to be encrypted with the handshake key? It is=20
not written in the <a href=3D"https://tlswg.github.io/tls13-spec/#rfc.secti=
on.4.4.3" target=3D"_blank">Finished message</a> section, it is not written=
 in the <a href=3D"https://tlswg.github.io/tls13-spec/#rfc.section.4.4" tar=
get=3D"_blank">authentication messages</a> parent section and it is not wri=
tten in the <a href=3D"https://tlswg.github.io/tls13-spec/#rfc.section.4" t=
arget=3D"_blank">handshake protocol</a> parent-parent section either. Only =
in the overview section.</p></li><li><p><strong>How to define application d=
ata in an application data message</strong>:
 It is not clear how &quot;real&quot; application data should appear in an=
=20
application data message. Encrypted handshakes and alerts are clearly=20
defined, but it takes some time to understand that &quot;real&quot; applica=
tion=20
data is written -- without structure or metadata -- into the <code>content<=
/code> field of a <code>TLSInnerPlaintext</code>
 message. The current definition is &quot;content: The cleartext of=20
TLSPlaintext.fragment&quot;. I think a better one would be &quot;content: t=
he byte
 encoding of a handshake or alert message, or the raw bytes of the=20
application&#39;s data to send&quot;.</p></li><li><p><strong>Unencrypted Ap=
plication Data</strong>: Does it say somewhere in the specification that re=
al application data should never appear in a <code>TLSPlaintext</code> mess=
age? At the moment the <code>application_data</code> content type is presen=
t in the declaration of a <code>TLSPlaintext</code> structure, see <a href=
=3D"https://tlswg.github.io/tls13-spec/#record-layer" target=3D"_blank">the=
 record layer section</a></p></li><li><p><strong>What alert to use</strong>=
: Sometimes it is not mentioned
 what level of alert should be used. For example: &quot;Because TLS 1.3=20
forbids renegotiation, if a server receives a ClientHello at any other=20
time, it MUST terminate the connection&quot;. I&#39;m guessing it should be=
 a=20
fatal alert, but this is open to interpretation.</p></li><li><p><strong>Why=
 force the signature algorithm extension</strong>: If=20
the client does not send a signature algorithm extension, why would the=20
server abort the handshake? It would make more sense to continue with=20
the server&#39;s prefered certificate. Especially since currently, most=20
webservers only possess one kind of certificate anyway.</p></li><li><p><str=
ong>Resumption_secret</strong>: The <a href=3D"https://tlswg.github.io/tls1=
3-spec/#rfc.section.7.1" target=3D"_blank">Key Schedule</a> section mention=
s a <code>resumption_secret</code>
 but nowhere in the spec does this keyword re-appear. It should be=20
mentioned somewhere that this is the secret to be used as PSK in a=20
future resumption handshake.</p></li><li><p><strong>Auth_modes</strong>: th=
ere is a single mention of &quot;auth_modes&quot; in the <a href=3D"https:/=
/tlswg.github.io/tls13-spec/#pre-shared-key-extension" target=3D"_blank">Pr=
e-Shared Key Extension</a> section. No definition of this variable is to be=
 found.</p></li><li><p><strong>Computing Finished keys</strong>: <a href=3D=
"https://tlswg.github.io/tls13-spec/#rfc.section.4.4.3" target=3D"_blank">T=
he section on how to compute Finished keys</a> should be in the <a href=3D"=
https://tlswg.github.io/tls13-spec/#cryptographic-computations" target=3D"_=
blank">cryptographic computation</a> section in my opinion. It should proba=
bly be added to the key schedule diagram as well.</p></li><li><p><strong>Co=
mputation of the Binders</strong>: From the <a href=3D"https://tlswg.github=
.io/tls13-spec/#psk-binder" target=3D"_blank">relevant section</a>: &quot;<=
em>Each
 entry in the binders list is computed as an HMAC over the portion of=20
the ClientHello up to and including the PreSharedKeyExtension.<wbr>identiti=
es
 field. That is, it includes all of the ClientHello but not the binder=20
list itself. The length fields for the message (including the overall=20
length, the length of the extensions block, and the length of the=20
=E2=80=9Cpre_shared_key=E2=80=9D extension) are all set as if the binder we=
re present</em>&quot;. There are several things that are unclear to me and =
I think they should be mentioned here: the fake binder should be of size <c=
ode>hash.size</code>,
 it should also say that the HMAC is computed over the handshake message
 containing the client hello, not only the client hello structure.</p></li>=
<li><p><strong>What binder key to use</strong>: It is not clear which=20
binder are to be used when doing a session resumption. The previous=20
handshake one or the new handshake one? The confusion comes from the=20
fact that the old handshake&#39;s <code>resumption_secret/PSK</code> is to =
be used in the new handshake, it could be thought that the old handshake&#3=
9;s <code>binder key</code> should be the one used in the new handshake as =
well. I think it would help if this <code>resumption_secret</code> is calle=
d something like <code>next_handshake.resumption_<wbr>secret</code>. Or mak=
e it clearer from which key schedule the <code>binder key</code> comes from=
.</p></li><li><p><strong>PSK and Cipher Suite</strong>: I think the followi=
ng fact
 should be emphasized in more places: the client needs to advertise the=20
single cipher suite they previously used when doing session resumption.=20
It could be added to the <code>cipher_suite</code> definition of the <a hre=
f=3D"https://tlswg.github.io/tls13-spec/#client-hello" target=3D"_blank">cl=
ient hello section</a> as well as in the <a href=3D"https://tlswg.github.io=
/tls13-spec/#resumption-and-psk" target=3D"_blank">overview of session resu=
mption</a>.</p></li><li><p><strong>Implicit Lengths in The Presentation Lan=
guage</strong>: <code>nested structures + implicit lengths =3D trouble</cod=
e>.
 It&#39;s easy to forget parsing/writing all the different &quot;sizes&quot=
; when they
 are not clearly stated in the spec. Why not just having a length field=20
in front of these variable-length vectors? It would add a bit of=20
overhead but would make things clearer in my opinion.</p></li><li><p><stron=
g>No Help For The Implementer</strong>: If you look at <a href=3D"http://ww=
w.noiseprotocol.org/noise.html" target=3D"_blank">the Noise Protocol Framew=
ork</a>,
 you can clearly see that the specifications were written with=20
implementers in mind: several states, and objects are defined. For=20
example, one way of implementing TLS is to have some sort of complex=20
state that will remember what state you are in the handshake, what is=20
the cryptographic context, etc... here the implementer has to decide=20
everything for himself.</p></li><li><p><strong>Whitelist Of State Transitio=
ns</strong>: There is nothing
 about whitelisting state transitions. In my opinion there should be a=20
list of allowed state transitions that the implementer could use. <a href=
=3D"https://gist.github.com/mimoo/779dcf8c44d80a2a34a1a2f2ed620711" target=
=3D"_blank">I made one here, I suspect it is still missing some states</a>.=
</p></li><li><p><strong>How to parse extensions</strong>: I think that <a h=
ref=3D"https://tlswg.github.io/tls13-spec/#iana-considerations" target=3D"_=
blank">IANA&#39;s consideration</a> section&#39;s table would be useful to =
have in the earlier <a href=3D"https://tlswg.github.io/tls13-spec/#extensio=
ns" target=3D"_blank">Extension section</a>
 (probably only the extensions included in the RFC should be listed).=20
Another useful pragraph: how to parse these extensions, making sure that
 different messages come with their own whitelist of extensions.</p></li><l=
i><p><strong>Language &lt;-&gt; Bytes Translation</strong>: There are no re=
al introduction/no clear section about this. I understand that everything i=
s in the <a href=3D"https://tlswg.github.io/tls13-spec/#presentation-langua=
ge" target=3D"_blank">Presentation Language</a>
 section but for someone who discovers the specification it takes some=20
time to find out that this section is about both the presentation=20
language and its byte encoding. A better introduction in this section as
 well as an emphasis on the byte representation in each of the=20
subsections of this section would in my opinion benefit the whole=20
document.</p></li><li><p><strong>Asymmetrical MUSTs</strong>: some &quot;MU=
ST&quot; keywords are not
 symmetrical. What is the point of them? For example, the spec says that
 as a client, you MUST use a unique <code>0x0303</code> value in the <code>=
legacy_version</code> field of the <a href=3D"https://tlswg.github.io/tls13=
-spec/#client-hello" target=3D"_blank">client hello</a>.
 But if you do not, there are no problems from the server side in the=20
specification and the client hello will still be well received. (Is this
 maybe for forward compatibility?)</p></li></ol>

<p>And finally, here are some minor nitpicks:</p>

<ol><li><p><strong>PSK extension</strong>: <strong>debatable</strong>, but =
I would rather have the <a href=3D"https://tlswg.github.io/tls13-spec/#pre-=
shared-key-extension" target=3D"_blank">PSK section</a> at the very end of =
the <a href=3D"https://tlswg.github.io/tls13-spec/#extensions" target=3D"_b=
lank">Extensions section</a> as it MUST be the final extension of a client =
hello. </p></li><li><p><strong>No Indication Of Encryption</strong>: This i=
s <strong>debatable</strong>,
 but I find it inelegant that there are no indication in a record that=20
it is encrypted. The implementation is supposed to keep track of it.</p></l=
i><li><p><strong>What PSK mode does a server support</strong>: I think the =
server should indicate what mode (<code>psk_dh_ke</code> or <code>psk_ke</c=
ode>) it supports in a <code>server_hello.psk_mode</code> extension or in t=
he <code>new_session_ticket</code> message. Like that the client would know=
 if it has to send a <code>key_share</code> or not in the resumption. Right=
 now, the only indication of a <code>psk_dh_ke</code> is that the server wi=
ll send a <code>key_share</code> even when presented with a PSK. <strong>th=
is is debatable</strong>.</p></li><li><p><strong>The Key Derivation</strong=
>: The key derivation section=20
is the least clear section in this specification. The diagrams kind of=20
help, but are limited by the RFC format... I have nothing else to say=20
here, the whole section is confusing to say the least. No solution=20
offered :o)</p></li></ol>
</article>
